We split order processing into services. Payments publishes `order.paid` and warehouse publishes `order.shipped` to a standard SQS queue (not FIFO), and this consumer in the order service keeps the order's status current. Please review it.

```ts
export async function handle(evt: OrderEvent) {
  await ddb.update({
    TableName: "orders",
    Key: { id: evt.orderId },
    UpdateExpression: "SET #s = :s, updatedAt = :t",
    ExpressionAttributeNames: { "#s": "status" },
    ExpressionAttributeValues: { ":s": evt.type === "order.paid" ? "PAID" : "SHIPPED", ":t": evt.ts },
  });
}
```

Orders can go paid -> shipped within seconds for digital goods. The orders table also has a `version` column that the admin tool increments when support edits an order.

Hey, quick review please. This is our checkout service (TypeScript). We have `lib/db` with an `OrderRepo` class and a `db.transact(ops)` helper that every other service uses for multi-record writes. This PR adds order creation:

```ts
export async function createOrder(userId: string, cart: Cart) {
  const orderId = newId();
  try {
    await client.put({ TableName: "orders", Item: { id: orderId, userId, total: cart.total } });
    for (const line of cart.lines) {
      await client.put({ TableName: "order_lines", Item: { orderId, ...line } });
    }
    await chargeCard(userId, cart.total);
    return orderId;
  } catch (err) {
    await client.delete({ TableName: "carts", Key: { userId } });
    log.error("order failed", err);
    throw err;
  }
}
```

The author's note: "cleaning up the cart on failure so users don't get stuck with a broken cart."

